  "query_preview": "..\nThrough Monday...\n\nIn the wake of the cold front yesterday, cooler conditions can be\nexpected over the area today with highs in the 70s most areas,\nwith a few locations in the southwest closer to 80 degrees.\nNorthwest winds of 15 to 30 mph can be expected near the Dakotas\nborder.\n\nUpper ridging builds over the region on Wednesday, with the ridge\naxis shifting east over the area during the day as a trough pushes\ninto the Pac NW. Temperatures are forecast to reach the 80s to \nlower 90s, a good 15 to 30 degrees above normal, and the warmest \nconditions of the year thus far. Southerly flow looks to increase \nover the area during the afternoon with winds gusting in the 20s \nand 30s mph, strongest over the western mountains and valleys and \nover the far east near the Dakotas border. Over the east, \nrelative humidity increases slightly with the southerly return \nflow, and at this time, don't anticipate needing any fire weather \nhighlites for eastern areas, but will continue to monitor for the \npotential. As the trough pushes east into the Northern Rockies,\nenergy lifting in from the southwest will bring isolated to scattered\nshowers and thunderstorms (20-40%) to western areas during the \nafternoon/evening. Modest CAPE (200-500 J/kg), steep lapse rates \nand inverted-V soundings suggest a wind threat, and SPC has a \nmarginal risk for strong to severe thunderstorms on our western \nperiphery. \n\nHeading into Wednesday night and Thursday, ensembles prog the\ntrough to develop into a low, lifting north and east, either\nacross the Canadian border, or a bit farther south through\nnorthern MT. An associated cold front is progged to push through\nthe area late Wednesday evening/night spreading gusty west to\nnorthwest winds and shower/thunderstorm chances east. The \nnorthern track would mean less shower and thunderstorm chances for\nour area into Thursday, with the southern track bringing the \ngreater potential. That being said, with the low developing to the\nnorth, tightening pressure gradients and increasing winds aloft \nwill bring the potential for gusty to strong winds for Thursday \nbehind the front. West to northwest winds with widespread gusts of\n30 to 50 mph are possible across most of the area, with gusts of \n40 to 60 mph possible along the western foothills (30-50% chance\nof 60 mph winds). While there remains a lot of uncertainty, \ndepending on the track of the low and precipitation forecast, the \ncombination of strong winds and low relative humidity could bring \nelevated fire weather conditions. Will need to monitor for the \npotential for high wind highlites as well.\n\nThe overall pattern becomes unsettled for Friday into early next\nweek as troughing remains over the intermountain west allowing for\nadditional shortwaves to track through the region bringing chances\nfor showers and thunderstorms. High temperatures are forecast to\nrange from the 60s to 70s most days from Thursday onward. STP",
